Z||000008||Will||06-10-2001||09:33 PM||||It can easily take 20-30 HP to drive a supercharger delivering decent boost and power.<P>20 HP * 749 W/HP = 14980 W<P>14980 W / 12 V = 1248 Amperes<P>You're not going to get much power out of this at all, even if you have a dedicated battery.<BR>It's just a scam.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||06-11-2001||09:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>It can easily take 20-30 HP to drive a supercharger delivering decent boost and power.<P>20 HP * 749 W/HP = 14980 W<P>14980 W / 12 V = 1248 Amperes<P>You're not going to get much power out of this at all, even if you have a dedicated battery.<BR>It's just a scam.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Or to flip that around, if their unit draws 50 Amps, and is 100% efficient, you get:<P>50Amps * 12V = 600W<P>600W / 749W/Hp = 0.80Hp<P>Not even a full horsepower, and that if it's 100% efficient.  Math is a harsh mistress.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DandRauto||06-11-2001||04:18 PM||||That last calculation is the power you put into the supercharge not what you get out of the engine.  Even with the mechanical one the 20 hp example to drive the supercharge generates more hp out.  But the increase hp out is from the increased air and fuel converted to HP.  So you get a net increase.<P>But even with this.  I don't think that there is any substantial gains with this electrical device.  I don't think that there is much of a pressure increase into the engine to create much hp.  So if you not creating much increased pressure then don't expect much of an output of the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000011||erikred||06-12-2001||05:17 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maninblack:<BR><B>I have hear from someone who i worked with<BR>about an electric supercharger,he read it<BR>sometimes ago from hot rod maz. does anyone recall?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know which one you are talking about. I saw a comercial for it on TV a few years ago. It was called the Superpac sold for about $1500 and made 5# boost. Grand Technologies, Inc. was the manufacturer, but they seem to be out of business. <A HREF="http://www.turbodyne.com" TARGET=_blank>www.turbodyne.com</A>  is currently planning on making a hydraulic/electric turbo, but they are having legal trouble right now. If anyone has more info on it I am interested.<p>[This message has been edited by erikred (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||06-12-2001||05:37 PM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000013||ebhan||06-14-2001||12:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DandRauto:<BR><B>That last calculation is the power you put into the supercharge not what you get out of the engine.  Even with the mechanical one the 20 hp example to drive the supercharge generates more hp out.  But the increase hp out is from the increased air and fuel converted to HP.  So you get a net increase.<P>Do anyone have any idea how many HPs gain one can expect from a super-charged engine for each sngle HP supplied to the turbo unit? (presumably more that one out for each one in -- if it is 3 for 1 the net gain should be 2 -- will it be better that that?).<P><BR>But even with this.  I don't think that there is any substantial gains with this electrical device.  I don't think that there is much of a pressure increase into the engine to create much hp.  So if you not creating much increased pressure then don't expect much of an output of the engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Iron Duke Wellingsley||06-14-2001||04:33 AM||||The idea of this little widget is not to double horsepower.  Instead, it helps the engine get air.  Instead of having a pressure drop in the intake, there is a slight positive pressure.  <P>Air flow can be thought of in a manner similar to Ohms law.  <P>E=IR<P>In this case, I is CFM, E is air pressure, and R is resistance.<P>So, I, or CFM, is <P>I=E/R  <P>And R, or resistance is <P>R=I/E<P>As you can see, E, or our pressure term is in the denominator.  When it goes from slight negative pressure to a slight positive pressure, the effect on R or our resistance term is dramatic going from a small positive value to infinity and the to a slight negative term.  <P>When it comes to denominators, math is also a tempermental b*tch .<P>I am not saying that this little intake fan will get you as much power as a supercharger.  Instead, I am suggesting that for each horsepower of "supercharger" output there is a diminishing benefit in terms of horsepower gain.<P>Put simply, that little widget could be a beneficial as your standard "port matching."  It could add 10 HP to an Iron Duke and more to a V6.  <P>Since it is electric, you can control it in very sophisticated ways.  It is a cool concept, but . . .<P>It probably is a 3/4 HP DC motor driving a fan.  If I had to guess, it looks like it could perhaps produce something like 300 CFM+ at 2" Hg (1 psi). This could make a measureable difference in an Iron Duke.  Somehow, I doubt higher flow engines like the 2.8 V6, a Quad 4, or an V8 would benefit from just one of them.<P>No one said you could not put an array of them on your car.  Who knows, 2 of them might make a V6 gain 15-20HP.<P>If I had $600 to throw away, I'd probably try that thing out on my Iron Duke.  I'll take all the ponies I can get . . .  <P>That I can switch the "boost" on and off sounds really cool to me.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Iron Duke Wellingsley (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||06-14-2001||08:02 AM||||Ok, the thing I left off is that it does take horsepower to compress air. I don't recall the formula right now, but 0.8 HP won't compress a lot of air.<P>$600 is a lot of money for 3 hp.<BR>If I wanted to spend money like that, I'd be into Hondas  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
